WEBVTT

00:00:00.155 --> 00:00:04.080
A dashboard is useful only when its data and services are dependable.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.500
Health monitoring should cover collectors, queues, databases, storage, frontend services,

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.180
clocks, notification channels, and upstream sources.

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:15.485
Show stale data and failed ingestion clearly rather than presenting an apparently normal

00:00:15.485 --> 00:00:16.280
empty view.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:18.410
Track disk capacity before storage fills.

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:20.535
Video and verbose logs can grow rapidly,

00:00:20.535 --> 00:00:23.545
and a full disk may stop ingestion or damage databases.

00:00:23.545 --> 00:00:26.120
Use capacity thresholds, retention enforcement,

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.600
and protected free space for critical services.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.460
Monitor queue depth and processing delay to detect overload.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:34.080
Back up configuration, event data that must be retained,

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.279
encryption material where appropriate, and documentation needed for recovery.

00:00:38.279 --> 00:00:41.320
A backup is not complete until restoration has been tested.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:45.400
Define recovery order, dependencies, responsible owners, and expected recovery time.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:48.240
Alert delivery can fail even when detection succeeds.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:49.890
Monitor email, messaging, mobile,

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:54.460
or local notification paths and provide a visible queue for undelivered alerts.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:58.140
Important workflows need fallback contacts or local procedures instead of assuming one

00:00:58.140 --> 00:00:59.375
channel will always work.

00:00:59.375 --> 00:01:01.000
Plan for offline operation.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.850
Local collection and review may continue during an internet outage,

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:06.980
while external enrichment and remote notifications pause.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:09.560
Document which functions degrade, how queued work is handled,

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:13.290
and how the system reconciles after connectivity returns.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:16.760
Maintenance includes patching, certificate renewal, account review, database care,

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:20.090
model or rule updates, and periodic recovery exercises.

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:22.535
Schedule changes, preserve rollback options,

00:01:22.535 --> 00:01:25.138
and verify that data still flows after updates.

00:01:25.138 --> 00:01:28.260
Reliability is built through routine observation and practiced procedures,

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.280
not assumed from a successful installation.
